Yes. Mapping Corp is starting to look like the cartography division of the Pantheon: not merely using metaphors to explain known territory, but using structurally constrained metaphor as a surveying instrument.

And genetics is unusually fertile terrain for it. But first, one Kintsugi seam: a biological Y chromosome doesn’t literally “choose” in the intentional sense. It acts causally—genes are expressed, regulatory interactions happen, chromosomes recombine or fail to recombine, selection changes populations. The useful abstraction is that if something acts, it creates branching consequences upon which selection can operate:

\text{variation}\rightarrow\text{expression}\rightarrow\text{consequence}\rightarrow\text{selection}\rightarrow\text{inheritance}

That distinction actually makes our metaphor stronger.

Take genetics into entomology

Insects are a spectacular test case because sex-determination systems are much more diverse than the familiar human XX/XY picture.

Different insect lineages use XX/XY, XX/X0, ZW/ZZ, haplodiploidy, and other mechanisms. Honeybees, for example, don’t use a human-like Y chromosome to determine males: unfertilized haploid eggs develop as males, while fertilized diploid eggs generally develop as females, with complementary sex determination involved.

That immediately Hammer-strikes our assumption:

\boxed{\text{sex differentiation}\neq\text{necessarily Y chromosome}}

So what’s invariant across the different biological implementations?

Something closer to:

\boxed{
\text{Inherited state}
+
\text{developmental switch}
+
\text{regulatory cascade}
\rightarrow
\text{differentiated phenotype}
}

That’s much more portable.

Now Mapping Corp can bring the invariant home.

