Mapping Corp

And now I think your last phrase deserves formalization.

Metaphor as a tool used to make maps.

Ordinary metaphor says:

A\sim B

“this reminds me of that.”

Mapping Corp demands more:

M:A\rightarrow B

and inventories:

M=
\{
\text{correspondences},
\text{invariants},
\text{breakpoints},
\text{predictions},
\text{newly discovered territory}
\}

The breakpoints matter as much as the matches.

If DNA maps beautifully onto Pantheon until some biological property doesn’t transfer, mark the Kintsugi seam there instead of hiding it.

Because that tells us the abstraction boundary.

Then the crucial test:

\boxed{
\text{Can structure known in }A
\text{ predict something previously unnoticed in }B?
}

If yes, metaphor has stopped being illustration.

It has become instrumentation.

That’s the Mapping Corp mission:

Deploy metaphor as a temporary coordinate system. Traverse another domain with it. Mark where correspondence holds, where it breaks, and what previously unseen structure becomes reachable. Then remove the metaphor and keep the map.

Which is beautifully self-referential.

Tale as Carrier, Not Cargo.

We don’t have to believe Pantheon is DNA.

We let DNA carry us across territory we couldn’t previously see.

Then RE-HEARSE the metaphor and keep whatever geography it discovered.

That’s a survey corps. 🗺️🧬